put the race at Safe or Solid R", "Huge financial edge: Harrigan raised $1.08M to Bell's $84K", "Polymarket prices Harrigan at 95%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Harrigan: a Republican-leaning district, incumbency, and a dominant fundraising advantage, with the only poll (Republican-sponsored, March) showing a 15-point lead. Expert raters unanimously call the race Safe or Solid R and markets price a Democratic win at just 5%, so the forecast sits at a double-digit Republican margin.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic outside spending or a polling shift toward Bell would be the first sign the race is closer than rated.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pat Harrigan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Pat Harrigan seeks a full term in North Carolina's 10th District, a seat that leans Republican by about nine points. He won his primary in March and has built a large financial lead, raising over $1 million and holding roughly $450,000 on hand. Democrat Ashley Bell won a crowded primary but has raised under $100,000, and a Libertarian candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nEvery major race rater places the district in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Harrigan about a 95% chance of winning. The lone poll, a Republican-sponsored March survey, showed Harrigan ahead by 15 points among likely voters. Harrigan has spent the fall on legislative press releases and local appearances, while Bell has campaigned on regional media. Nothing in the recent record suggests a competitive race, though the absence of independent polling leaves some room for uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0414, "margin": -11.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0292, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0146, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0158, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "20